A||||13||MooCow||What kills a clutch??||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||07-25-2001||11:37 AM||||Well as some of you may know I am considering trading my car or just selling it to get an auto.  The only reason is I hate shifting cause I thihk the clutch is going to go out and then I'm screwed.  I have brand new clutch with about 2,700 miles on it.  Brand new slave and master too.  All was done at the Fiero Factory by Dave.  Also Dave turned my timming some so it is not at factory.  He said he did it for in the higher RPM's it would perform better and shift better, but for me I am too afraid to rev my car past 3ish.  I have a 3.4 with the 4:10 gears and with the way I drive I bet a 4cylinder might beat me.  So what really kills a clutch???????????<BR>Someone knock some sense into me.<P>                                Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||5
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||07-25-2001||12:06 PM||||only time you will kill it is when you are releasing it or have it slightly disengaged. Just shift smooth between gears. Don't worry about getting on the engine when you are not pushing the clutch. I get my stock 2.8 to 5,000 rpm sometimes higher <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Stock style replacement clutch 30,000-40,000 miles ago and it is still holding strong. I have an Isuzu 5 speed. You can hear and feel when you are burning your clutch. Just smooth easy release. If you have the engine spinning fast don't forget the trans is stopped so the clutch must cause friction to get everything spinning at the same speed. <P>Just don't worry and please stop treating it like an old lady car. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Give it some power full throttle and have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||12:18 PM||||doing burnouts. resting your foot on pedal while you drive. waiting at a light with car in gear and pedal in. holding your car on a hill with it instead of brakes. pulling heavy loads. Thats assuming it was installed and adjusted correctly and that probably not the problem in your case.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mbramble||07-25-2001||12:28 PM||||I have a '91 S-10 (2.8, 5 spd) that I bought new and now has 124K miles on it.  I just had the clutch replaced about 8K miles ago.  It didn't bother me to pay $400 to have it replaced after 10 years of all kinds of driving (mostly city but lots of highway).<P>Don't try to burn rubber (too often!) and keep your foot off the clutch except when shifting and it should last a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||07-25-2001||12:33 PM||||I dont ever keep my foot on the clutch wile driving.  Also I never downshift, plus if I am at a light I keep it in nuetral till it turns green.  I always thought that if I reved it passed 4ish then my clutch would not grab and then go out on me.  On my Ranger I rev it to 4-5 grand, but for some reason on the Fiero I am so afraid that the stock V6 clutch is going to fail me.  Now at least I know I was wrong and maybe will drive this car how it should be driven.||||reg||10
Z||000005||StuGood||07-25-2001||12:52 PM||||You guys, <B>pleaeeese</B> stop talking sense to this poor man! MooCow, don't listen to these baaad people!  There's no reason for you to suffer living with that big bad ol' 3.4!  <P>You're in luck!  Uncle Stu will trade you straight across, his wonderful 2.8 SE Auto for your awful 3.4 with that nasty 4.10 manual transaxle  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">! Just hurry down to Old Cowtown (Wichita) before old Stu changes his generous mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  J/K, of course!   ||||reg||12
Z||000006||1FST2M6||07-25-2001||01:45 PM||||OH NO!!! MAN...  if it's a recent clutch with a properly prepared flywheel (i'm sure they did it all corretly) tach it to 2000 and dump the clutch stand on the gas to 6000 slam 2nd... rinse repeat.  your really missing out on the go that bigger engine has. slipping the clutch in traffic or a leaking rear main seal will kill the clutch. (or too much nitrous and over power the pressure plate) but spirited driving will kill it in 20k miles. by then you'll be bored and want a V8 anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||07-25-2001||02:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>waiting at a light with car in gear and pedal in</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe if the clutch system doesn't hold.<P>If you have the car in gear w/clutch in and it doesn't go anywhere w/brakes released (granted you are on a flat road) then it isn't going to wear the clutch because the clutch is <I>not engaged</I>.<P>It can be unsafe if you were rear ended and your foot comes off the clutch and brake.  Chances are the engine would stall, but you might bump into the car ahead of you.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||JayUSA77||07-25-2001||02:26 PM||||hey man your car looks great and you say it has a 3.4L so it has some balls to back up its looks so use them.  Why dump a bigger engine if your afraid of burning the clutch.  Just drive it normal as good as you can.  I bring my RPM in my last little 4cyl to about 2500 or 2200 when i took off and my STOCK clutch lasted to 88,000miles. And yes it held DEAD strong to the last mile.  And trust me guys I abused the clutch Major.  I did burnouts,(OR at least tried with my inline 4) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, raced every import that would race me back, and just pounded gears for the hell of it.  the way i look at it is i knew the clutch was going to die eventually so i might as well have fun before it did.  and when my car did blow the clutch i installed my own in about 1 and a half weeks and it works PERFECT NOW.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So have fun and go cook some rice. <BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steve Normington||07-25-2001||04:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88CoupeV6:<BR><B>I have an Isuzu 5 speed.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What parts did you need to install the Isuzu into your Fiero? This is a project that I am looking at to replace my Muncie.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88CoupeV6||07-25-2001||05:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B> What parts did you need to install the Isuzu into your Fiero? This is a project that I am looking at to replace my Muncie.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The shifter unit from the console, the shifter cables, trans mounts & the slave cylinder. If you can find a 4 cyl parts car just take all the parts from that. I think you would be happy with that trans. I have one of each and the Muncie is not as good as the Isuzu in my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fred88GT||07-25-2001||06:15 PM||||Ken<P>I was at Menards twice this week and both times there was a yellow GT in the parking lot. Is that yours? We should get together<BR>and check out each others cars.I also have an<BR>opinion about clutches I could give you.<P>Fred<P>85 GT 4 Speed<BR>88 GT Auto||||reg||1
Z||000012||MooCow||07-25-2001||06:44 PM||||Hey fred88GT yes that is my car.  I work in the flooring section there.  Yes lets get together.  I am off now until sunday, so if you are free drop me a line and we can get together.<BR>Wow now that I know this about a clutch am I starting to like a stick a bit more.<P>                              Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||09:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B> Maybe if the clutch system doesn't hold.<P>If you have the car in gear w/clutch in and it doesn't go anywhere w/brakes released (granted you are on a flat road) then it isn't going to wear the clutch because the clutch is <I>not engaged</I>.<P>It can be unsafe if you were rear ended and your foot comes off the clutch and brake.  Chances are the engine would stall, but you might bump into the car ahead of you.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I dont agree. even if its disengaged with the pedal completely down, there is some degree of contact. Just like brake pad against the brake rotor. Its not enough to move the car or even slow the engine but its still generating friction heat. Thats not to mention your wearing out the throw out bearing 10 times as fast holding the pedal down.<P>||||reg||1
